    <div class=3D"s-prose js-post-body" itemprop=3D"text">
<blockquote>
<p>What is the cleanest way to ssh and run multiple commands in Bash?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I recommend using this escaping function. The function takes one argumen=
t - a function to escape. Then <code>sshqfunc</code> outputs <code>declare =
-f</code> of the function and then outputs a string that will call the func=
tion with <code>"$@"</code> arguments properly quoted. Then the whole is <c=
ode>"%q"</code> quoted and <code>bash -c</code> is added. In case the remot=
e does not have bash, you could change bash to sh.</p>
<pre class=3D"lang-bash s-code-block"><code data-highlighted=3D"yes" class=
=3D"hljs language-bash"><span class=3D"hljs-function"><span class=3D"hljs-t=
itle">sshqfunc</span></span>() { <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">echo</span> =
<span class=3D"hljs-string">"bash -c <span class=3D"hljs-subst">$(printf <s=
pan class=3D"hljs-string">"%q"</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<span cl=
ass=3D"hljs-subst">$(declare -f <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<span class=3D=
"hljs-variable">$@</span>"</span>)</span>; <span class=3D"hljs-variable">$1=
</span> \"\$@\""</span>)</span>"</span>; };
</code></pre>
<p>Then define a function with the work you want to do on the remote. The f=
unction is defined normally, so it will be properly "clean". You can test s=
uch function locally. After defining, properly escaped function is passed t=
o the remote.</p>
<pre class=3D"lang-bash s-code-block"><code data-highlighted=3D"yes" class=
=3D"hljs language-bash"><span class=3D"hljs-function"><span class=3D"hljs-t=
itle">work</span></span>() {
   <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">ls</span>
   <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">pwd</span>
   <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">echo</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">"S=
ome other command"</span>
}

ssh host@something <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<span class=3D"hljs-subst">=
$(sshqfunc work)</span>"</span>
</code></pre>
<p>Passing You can also pass arguments, and they will be passed to your fun=
ction as positional arguments. The right next argument after the function w=
ill be assigned to <code>$0</code> - usually a placeholder like <code>--</c=
ode> or <code>_</code> is used to separate arguments from call.</p>
<pre class=3D"lang-bash s-code-block"><code data-highlighted=3D"yes" class=
=3D"hljs language-bash"><span class=3D"hljs-function"><span class=3D"hljs-t=
itle">work</span></span>() {
   file=3D<span class=3D"hljs-variable">$1</span>
   num=3D<span class=3D"hljs-variable">$2</span>
   <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">ls</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<sp=
an class=3D"hljs-variable">$file</span>"</span>
   <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">echo</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">"n=
um is <span class=3D"hljs-variable">$num</span>"</span>
}

ssh host@something <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<span class=3D"hljs-subst">=
$(sshqfunc work)</span>"</span> -- /this/file 5
</code></pre>
<p>But note that arguments should also be properly quoted if there are any =
magic characters:</p>
<pre class=3D"lang-bash s-code-block"><code data-highlighted=3D"yes" class=
=3D"hljs language-bash">ssh host@something <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<sp=
an class=3D"hljs-subst">$(sshqfunc work)</span>"</span> -- <span class=3D"h=
ljs-string">"<span class=3D"hljs-subst">$(printf <span class=3D"hljs-string=
">"%q"</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<span class=3D"hljs-variable">$v=
ar1</span>"</span> <span class=3D"hljs-string">"<span class=3D"hljs-variabl=
e">$var2</span>"</span>)</span>"</span>
</code></pre>
